# DURABLE REPRO — B4: `for x in &v: x = ...` over a SCALAR-element collection
# silently loses the write.
#
# WRONG TODAY (measured at HEAD, Rust gg, C backend): prints
#     1 / 2 / 3
# The loop body runs, the assignment is accepted, and nothing reaches the
# collection. `gg check` is clean — silent-wrong-output.
#
# INTENDED (asserted below): 11 / 12 / 13. `for x in &v` binds each element as a
# mutable borrow, which is the whole point of the sigil (D31 full-strict: `&`
# means the write goes through).
#
# ⚠ CORRECTION — THIS HEADER USED TO CLAIM "the resource-element twins of this
# shape already write through", citing
# `cow_for_amp_resource_elem_writethrough.gg`. THAT IS NOT A TWIN. That fixture
# writes a FIELD through the element place (`x.v = x.v + 100`), which is a
# different mechanism; this one REBINDS THE WHOLE BINDING (`x = x + 10`).
# Measured on a controlled pair — same type, same `&`, same loop — the field
# write reaches the collection (`101`) and the whole-binding rebind does not
# (`1`). ⇒ THE DISCRIMINATOR IS THE MUTATION SHAPE, NOT THE ELEMENT TYPE, and
# the whole-binding rebind writes through at NO element type: `int` here,
# `Vector[int]`, struct and `String` alike. So this is not "the by-value half
# of a half-shipped rule" — it is a class with no working precedent in the
# tree, filed as `todo/t1404`.
#
# ⊕ `todo/t0045` (the `String` cell of this same rebind shape) fixed only the
# MEMORY-SAFETY half: `String` used to double-free here and now behaves like
# `int` — accepted, memory-safe, write silently lost. It did NOT move this
# fixture in either direction, which was measured, not assumed.
#
# ORACLE: out of ggdef's phase-0 subset ("`for &`/`for !` iteration is
# Increment B2"), so this row is pinned against the RULE, not against a third
# implementation. Noted here rather than left implicit — an out-of-subset shape
# owes an explicit note (Core #9).
#
# The written value depends on the element read (`x + 10`), so a const-fold
# cannot elide it, and the three distinct results prove every iteration landed
# rather than one write being smeared.
void main():
    Vector[int] v = [1, 2, 3]
    for x in &v:
        x = x + 10
    print(v[0])
    print(v[1])
    print(v[2])
